/*
 *+
 * FUNCTION NAME:
 * doC_X
 *
 * INVOCATION:
 * doC_X(motor)
 *
 * PARAMETERS: (">" input, "!" modified, "<" output)
 * (>) motor (int)          Motor to use
 *
 * FUNCTION VALUE:
 * (void) Returns 'void' and hence has no STATUS value
 *
 * PURPOSE:
 * Send control-X
 *
 * DESCRIPTION:
 * Sends a control-X command to a motor.  Control-X clears the error
 * bits in the status register.
 *
 * EXTERNAL VARIABLES:
 *
 *
 * PRIOR REQUIREMENTS:
 *
 *
 * DEFICIENCIES:
 *
 *
 * HISTORY:
 * Jul 1, 2001      Initial Version                 (rwolff@noao.edu)
 *
 *-
 */
/*
 *+
 * FUNCTION NAME:
 * cmd
 *
 * INVOCATION:
 * cmd(motor)
 *
 * PARAMETERS: (">" input, "!" modified, "<" output)
 * (>) motor (int)          Motor to use
 *
 * FUNCTION VALUE:
 * (void) Returns 'void' and hence has no STATUS value
 *
 * PURPOSE:
 * Interactive control of motor
 *
 * DESCRIPTION:
 * This command allows the user to send any OMS command sequence to a
 * motor, thus providing debugging capability at the lowest possible
 * level.  The command prompts with ">Md" where 'd' is the motor number.
 * To quit the endless prompt-command loop, press the escape key in
 * place of a command.  Commands are examined by looking at the first
 * letter of the input line.  If the command is 'WY', or starts with
 * an 'R' or a 'Q', a response is expected and will be printed.  Otherwise,
 * the input string is sent verbatim to the motor.
 *
 * There are two sets of special commands.  One is the * '"' (double quote)
 * and control-R, which repeat * the previous motor command.  The other set
 * is the '`' (backquote) and control-W which execute the 'mstatus(motor, 0)'
 * command, thereby allowing a quick way to get status.
 *
 * This routine does _not_ turn on motor power before it starts.  Therefore,
 * the user must do so ( motorOn(motor) and motorOff(motor) afterward).
 *
 * EXTERNAL VARIABLES:
 *
 *
 * PRIOR REQUIREMENTS:
 *
 *
 * DEFICIENCIES:
 *
 *
 * HISTORY:
 * Jul 1, 2001      Initial Version                 (rwolff@noao.edu)
 *
 *-
 */
/*
 *+
